>        as to why they are not present, bearing in mind they are PALCODE
>        routines.

The VMS and Tru64 PALcode is very different and reflects the differing
backgrounds of the two OSes.  I know the VAX has queuing instructions
much like the PALcode, I wouldn't be surprised if there are probe
instructions too.

BSD Unix had to run on other platforms and it generally relies on
C code for linked list management.  Besides, you often have to do
other work beyond the list manipulation and have to take out a SMP
lock anyway.  At least, that's the line I'd expect to hear.  The Unix
PALcode has things like an instruction for swapping the processor's
interrupt level to implement the "spl(s)" calls that run throughput Unix.

So basically, the PALcode is the OS-specific low level glue needed to
ease matching the OS to the hardware. It's not really designed with
portability in mind.  You'd run into similar problems porting to other
processors.

The architecture manual at:

http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/semiconductor/literature/dsc-library.html

may be the document that goes into gory detail about the common and
OS-specific PALcode pieces.
