If it couldn't be opened, it is most likely missing, so relacing
it would be a start.

>is osf_boot attach to  /var for logging?

The "for logging" part of the question doesn't make much sense
to me, but "osf_boot" is in the root file system, not /var.

>how do i run a fsck on local disks after booting from an install cdrom?

Once you get the installation CDROM to get you a single-user shell,
simply type "fsck" and the special device of the raw device you
want to check.  I think modern versions create the special files
as they boot.  For older versions you may need to use /dev/MAKEDEV
to create the special files.

>what are my options (other than reinstall)?

1.  Boot the installation CDROM to single-user as you noted.

2.  Use fsck to check the UFS file systems.  For AdvFS mounting
    the file system will replay the log.  Start with the root
    and work through /usr and /var then any local file systems
    that might be suspect.  For AdvFS domains you may need to
    to create the directory structure by hand.  Recent versions
    have advscan for this.

3.  Restore missing files from the installation media or a recent
    backup.

