    I would imagine not, simply because of the huge security risk. I believe
it could be done with portmap on NetBSD, but I have little experience with
DG-UX. Why not write an interactive login script that only allows a user to
connect with telnet, and not actually get to a shell prompt. Or more simply,
set the password file to run telnet instead of a shell. Then they will be
logged out when they quit telnet (I believe, I never actually tried it).
Being a telnet gateway (with packet mapping) is just too much of a pain, and
it will probably slow down the traffic going through it if you have
portmap/packet filter running.
    One good configuration I have seen which offers decent security, set up
all the target telnet machines to only allow connections from the gateway
machine. So you must telnet to the gateway then telnet to the target
machine, you can't skip the gateway. Unfortunately this solution is subject
to security holes by IP spoofing as it stands.


