In article <81u04d$ojq$1@fleetstreet.Austria.EU.net>, Joe Leherbauer <joe@takefive.co.at> writes:
> I always thought it doesn't.
> 
> But GCC emits a "divqu" instruction for this code:
> 
> unsigned long a, b, c;
> c = a / b;

It does not.  But GCC can claim it's generating just about anything.  

DEC Fortran claims to generate .displ, even though there's no such directive.
VAX Macro recognizes popl and generates the code that used to be in the 
macro library, but doesn't have to look up the macro (it's built into
the assembler, and it's no longer in the macro library).

What opcode does it actually assemble to?  (Try running /debug and displaying
the INST display in screen mode).  There are several instruction-like
macros in starlet.mlb and lib.mlb, and the assembler recognize several
stylized instruction forms, but not divqu.

GCC may be running code through it's own assembler (gas?), but more likley
it's generating the code internally.  It's almost certainly not dependent on
the formerly additional cost Macro 64 assembler.

