> I think we would be better off donating some beer and pizza to the 
> XFree86 developer team and talk them into porting XFree86 to Tru64 Unix.
> This really would solve the graphics card problem under Tru64 Unix.

I second that.  Either that or we need to make use of the source code that's
out there and come up with a device driver and DDX for Tru64 Unix for a
few *common* PCI cards.  I looked into what would be involved in writing
a video device driver + a DDX about a year ago, and unfortunately video
drivers are more difficult than many of the other types of device drivers.
If you've never written a device driver before, starting with a video driver
is a tough path to choose.

I have the complete Device Drivers kit for Digital Unix (circa 4.0b days)
courtesy of Dr. Tom Blinn of Compaq (thanks again, Tom!).  The kit is weighty
and probably very good from the point of view of an expert on device drivers,
but from *my* point of the view the examples are a little thin/skeletal,
especially in the are of video drivers & DDXes.  Getting even one well-written
and well-commented driver + DDX in the OpenSource domain would help immensely.

As I recall from my investigation last year, Tru64 Unix uses the typical
BSD probe and attach calls for device discovery, while Linux uses a single
call that does both (I don't remember what the call is, but I want to say
it's slattach()).  In any case, this means that the Open/Net/Free BSD source
code would probably server as a better example for would-be Tru64 Unix
driver writers than the Linux code would, though both are definitely useful.

I'm not sure what Compaq's policy is on employees writing code in their "spare"
time, but perhaps we should offer beer/pizza/whatever to one or more of the 
Compaq device driver engineers, in return for some OpenSource code for a
common PCI card.

Tim
