In article <7vq428$bbf$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, <sridhara@my-deja.com> wrote:

>We have a file system that is built on three raid sets all of which use
>18GB drives (I used addvol command for subsequent raid sets). Now If I
>buy a bunch of 36 GB drives for the ESA 10000 cabinet and build a raid
>set on it, can I use addvol to add this set to the existing domain? The
>cabinet is attached to a 8400 running DU 4.0E

I do not know anything about hardware, so assuming there are no
hardware issues in this question, then the answer is "Yes".  

From an AdvFS perspective, AdvFS doesn't care what size your volumes
are.  They can be large or small, they can be single disks, they can be
stripped, then can be mirrored, they can be RAID (any RAID number you
choose), then can be LSM volumes, they can be solid state disks, and
you have have any mix of these in any sizes you wish (with the
restriction that no single volume can appear to AdvFS as more than 1
terabyte in size; but you can have several 1TB volumes until you reach
the 16TB file system limit).

It is all just goes into the AdvFS domain as a pool of disk blocks to
be given out to files.



