In article <7v9s78$o32$1@nsl.nts.nnov.ru>, "Alexei Sorokin" <Alexei.Sorokin@rcm.nnov.ru> writes:
|> hi everybody
|> can i  enable/disable  range of ports by one line??
|> 
|> e.x.
|> 
|> between host <ip address>[tcp ports from 1024 to 65535] and any [tcp ports
|> from 1024 to 65535] accept;
|> 
|> ???
|> actually, need to enable ICQ

Screend (at least, the original version that I wrote) does not
have a syntax to express arbitrary ranges of tcp ports.  However,
the specific example that you cite could be expressed as:

	between host <ip_address> tcp port reserved
		and any tcp port reserved reject;
	between host <ip_address> and any accept;

because "reserved" means ports 0-1023.

You *might* be able to use this:

	between host <ip_address> tcp port-not reserved
		and any tcp port-not reserved accept;

but I can't remember if the code actually knows how to deal
with "port-not reserved" in the way that you would hope.

-Jeff



