In article <38284353.85700EAD@psi.ch>,
	Mark Koennecke <Mark.Koennecke@psi.ch> writes:
> High,
> 
> I have a curious problem here. We are using DigitalUnix workstations
> running DU4.0d as dedicated instrument computers for neutron scattering
> instruments. This essentially means users stay logged in
> for extended periods of time such as days and weeks. Now, first
> everything is fine. But in some stage
> any start of a X-application results in a cannot connect error message.
> I checked if all the X-processes
> were running but that was the case. Login out and in again usually
> solves the problem.

Looks like something might be messing around with your X authorization
mechanism.  There are two forms of authorization, "xhost" (the
original one) and "xauth" (more recent).

Try:
	xhost +

and see if your applications can connect.  This command simply removes
all authorization from your system (allowing other people to open
windows on your screen, read your screen, etc.) -- not a very secure
environment.

If this works, check out the man pages for "xauth" for information on
how to carry your authorization tickets from account to account.

