> I have a curious problem here. We are using DigitalUnix workstations
> running DU4.0d as dedicated instrument computers for neutron scattering
> instruments. This essentially means users stay logged in
> for extended periods of time such as days and weeks. Now, first
> everything is fine. But in some stage
> any start of a X-application results in a cannot connect error message.
> I checked if all the X-processes
> were running but that was the case. Login out and in again usually
> solves the problem.

You don't have a script that's cleaning out /tmp and /var/tmp, and possibly
accidentally removing the /tmp/.X11-unix/X0 socket, do you?  If that file gets
removed, X just isn't going to work correctly.

The Digital Unix implementation of the SVR4 filesystem is very nice, but
one mistake they made is that some applications put their sockets and pid
files in /tmp.  There are better places in the filesystem for those things
than /tmp.

Tim
