Not at all.  The Digital C compiler is fully functional as shipped. The C++
compiler, however, does require a separate license to operate.

> If so, then I *do* want gcc.  However, I've heard that gcc for the Alpha
> is not as optimized as DEC's C compiler.
> 
Correct.  Even the newest egcs compiler cannot provide anywhere close to the
optimization provided by the DEC C compiler. And the DEC C compiler keeps
getting better. With Tru64 UNIX 4.0E many applications run faster (up to 30%)
than they did with the previous compiler. Diagnostics were greatly enhanced,
covering many more error/warning/info cases, and making existing messages
much easier to understand. With the port to Alpha/Linux, the compiler now
understands most, if not all, compiler flags used by gcc, so it is now a
drop-in replacement for gcc/egcs on Alpha/Linux platforms.

I see no need to use gcc/egcs.

> Then again, gcc is more "portable" in that I have the same version
> (2.7.2.2) on all my UNIX boxes so I can compile the same code
> everywhere.
> 
I've never had problems compiling with DEC C. A few compiler flags
may need changing, but I've never seen an application fail to compile
under DEC C that did work with gcc. When I find hardcoded compiler
calls in a Makefile, substituting 'cc -std1' for 'gcc -Wall' is
usually all it takes to get a clean build.

> I still think I'm interested in gcc for Tru64 UNIX...
> 
Your choice.  I used gcc for a long time on DEC ULTRIX, because it was
still evolving long after ULTRIX stopped.  But with Tru64 UNIX, I see
no need to keep using it. YMMV.

